<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="2.16.840.1.113883.10.20.22.2.9" extension="2014-06-09"/>
    <code code="51847-2" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Evaluation + Plan note"/>
    <title>Assessment and Plan</title>
    <text><list listType="ordered"><item><content styleCode="Bold">Benign prostatic hyperplasia with refractory retention: informed decision to defer surgery (N40.1)</content>. He has the capacity to decide, understands that an operation is recommended for refractory retention and that HoLEP offers the best chance of voiding without a catheter, and understands the risks of a long-term catheter (repeated infection, stones, bleeding, and bladder damage) and that a second trial may fail. His choice to defer any procedure fits his values and is a reasonable one. [S]<list><item>Keep the indwelling catheter, changed every four weeks.</item><item>Continue tamsulosin and finasteride.</item><item>Second trial without catheter after three to four months of finasteride.</item><item>If it fails, or if infections recur, bleeding, stones, or kidney harm develop: reconsider prostatic artery embolization first, then HoLEP; urology will see him again at that point.</item><item>Herbal preparation may continue: buy it from a supplier whose products are tested for lead, mercury, and arsenic; a blood lead level was offered and he declined.</item></list></item><item><content styleCode="Bold">Goals of care</content>. What matters most to him is to keep writing and teaching, to stay at home among the people he loves, to keep a clear mind, and to avoid an operation unless there is no gentler choice. He wants honest information. He would accept treatment that could restore him to that life, including antibiotics and a hospital stay. [S]<list><item>Name a health care agent and record his wishes at the wellness visit in six weeks.</item></list></item></list><paragraph>Protocol monitoring: items the monitoring protocol calls for at this visit. [S]</paragraph><table><thead><tr><th>Domain</th><th>Measure</th><th>Interval</th><th>Status</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Survey</td><td>IPSS symptom and quality-of-life scores</td><td>Baseline and every follow-up visit once voiding</td><td>Not indicated</td><td>Not scored while catheterized</td></tr><tr><td>Imaging</td><td>Post-void residual by bladder scan</td><td>With retention, at each trial without catheter, and after it</td><td>Not indicated</td><td>Catheter in place</td></tr><tr><td>Laboratory</td><td>Creatinine, eGFR, and electrolytes</td><td>With retention or suspected obstructive uropathy; after drainage and at each trial without catheter</td><td>Up to date</td><td>Normal four weeks ago; next with the second trial without catheter</td></tr><tr><td>Self-management</td><td>Catheter care and infection prevention (closed system, bag below the bladder, daily washing, change every four weeks, no routine antibiotics)</td><td>Every visit while catheterized</td><td>Completed today</td><td>No infection since March; changed on schedule by the clinic nurse</td></tr><tr><td>Medication safety</td><td>Tamsulosin effects: dizziness, orthostatic symptoms, falls, nasal stuffiness</td><td>4 to 12 weeks after starting, then every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>No dizziness or falls at ten weeks; response to be judged at the next trial</td></tr><tr><td>Medication safety</td><td>Finasteride effects: breast tenderness or enlargement, reduced libido; PSA halved on treatment</td><td>3 to 6 months after starting, then periodically</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Eight weeks on finasteride; no breast tenderness or other effects</td></tr><tr><td>Laboratory</td><td>PSA</td><td>Baseline when it would change management; new baseline about 6 months after starting finasteride, doubled for interpretation</td><td>Due</td><td>New baseline about six months after starting finasteride</td></tr><tr><td>Referral</td><td>Surgical decision after a failed trial without catheter</td><td>Shared decision after counseling</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Informed decision to defer surgery, with clear criteria to reconsider; urology informed</td></tr><tr><td>Medication safety</td><td>Herbal preparation safety</td><td>When a herbal or traditional remedy is taken</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Continued with his agreement to buy from a tested supplier; blood lead level offered and declined</td></tr></tbody></table><paragraph>Goal progress:</paragraph><list><item>Bladder drained and kidney function back to normal within two weeks. Outcome by 2026-03-03: Creatinine 1.42 mg/dL at presentation, 1.18 mg/dL after two weeks of drainage; no hydronephrosis [S]</item><item>No catheter-associated infection, or prompt treatment if one occurs. Outcome by 2026-03-31: One febrile catheter-associated infection at four weeks, treated in clinic for 7 days without admission; resolved [S]</item><item>A treatment decision that he understands and that fits his values, after a geriatric assessment, by about ten weeks. Outcome by 2026-04-28: Chose to continue the catheter with tamsulosin and finasteride and to try again without the catheter after three to four months, deferring surgery; recorded in his own words [S]</item><item>Voiding without a catheter, with a post-void residual under 300 mL, by about five months. In progress; target 2026-07-14 [S]</item><item>Goals of care and a health care agent recorded. In progress; target 2026-06-09 [S]</item></list><paragraph>Guideline basis: Management of Lower Urinary Tract Symptoms Attributed to Benign Prostatic Hyperplasia: AUA Guideline, Part I (Initial Work-up and Medical Management) and Part II (Surgical Evaluation and Treatment) (2021); Management of Lower Urinary Tract Symptoms Attributed to Benign Prostatic Hyperplasia (BPH): AUA Guideline Amendment 2023 (2023); AUA White Paper on Nonneurogenic Chronic Urinary Retention: Consensus Definition, Treatment Algorithm, and Outcome End Points (2017); Lead, mercury, and arsenic in US- and Indian-manufactured Ayurvedic medicines sold via the Internet (2008); CMID: Clinical Monitoring Intelligence Database, Benign prostatic hyperplasia monograph (2026)</paragraph><paragraph>Follow-up: Annual wellness visit in six weeks; review with the second trial without catheter in about eleven weeks. Next scheduled visit 2026-06-09: subsequent annual wellness visit. [S]</paragraph></text>

  </section>
